      en: "Kota Tua, or Jakarta's Old Town, is the historical heart of the Indonesian capital, formerly known as Batavia. This district contains the highest concentration of Dutch colonial architecture in the country, centered around Fatahillah Square. The old City Hall, now the Jakarta History Museum, stands as the centerpiece of what was once the 'Queen of the East.' Visitors can explore 17th-century canals, the iconic red-brick Wayang Museum, and the historic Cafe Batavia. Kota Tua represents the centuries-long colonial encounter between the Dutch East India Company (VOC) and the indigenous people of the archipelago. Recent revitalization efforts have transformed the square into a vibrant public space while working to preserve its status as an international heritage site. Geography K8 — Colonial Urbanism and Maritime History."
